Done. Both files rewritten, verification green.

## Files written (only these two)

**`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/scorer/same-owner-ambiguity.vitest.ts`**
- Owner is now resolved from the generated bridge data at test time: `resolveOwnedBridge()` filters `COMMAND_BRIDGES` for `id === 'command-spec-kit-plan'` **with** an `ownerSkill`, throws a loud error if the data stops carrying an owner, and yields `BRIDGE_ID` / `OWNER_SKILL` (currently `command-spec-kit-plan` → `system-spec-kit`). Non-owner is the real skill `sk-code`. No ids are guessed from shape.
- Kept the three intents: command row leads / owner leads → not ambiguous (both orders, plus `ambiguousWith` undefined on both); two unrelated skills within 0.05 → ambiguous; command row + non-owner skill → ambiguous.

**`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/scorer/doctor-ordering.vitest.ts`**
- Renamed `'leaves skills and non-doctor commands alone'` → `'leaves skills alone while every command needs its own phrase'`, and it now states the widened rule: `sk-git` (skill, lexical-only) stays `true`; `memory:save` (command, lexical-only, non-doctor) flips to `false`; `create:agent` (command, non-doctor, `explicit_author` rawScore 1) stays `true` → `[true, false, true]`. Added a durable WHY comment (everyday-word phrases; all command families, not doctor only).

## The "third unrelated skill ties" case — what it returns now

With the real bridge/owner pair, `ambiguity.ts` returns:
- `isAmbiguousTopTwo` → **true**
- `command-spec-kit-plan` → `ambiguousWith: ['sk-code']`
- `sk-code` → `ambiguousWith: ['command-spec-kit-plan']`
- owner `system-spec-kit` → `ambiguousWith: undefined`

Coherent, so I asserted it: `answersSameRoute` drops the owner from the cluster (the top command row's own workflow, "the same answer written twice"), while the rival skill is a genuine second route, leaving a size-2 cluster. The old failure was exactly the missing owner link — the hand-authored `command-spec-kit` bridge carries no `ownerSkill`, so `system-spec-kit` was not excluded and the row reported `['system-spec-kit','sk-code']`.

